// run
// #3165: `checkSliceExpr` (compiler/checkmatch.bit) tested the slice
// receiver's type for `TypeKind.Prim && prim == 11` without first defaulting
// an untyped string LITERAL receiver — a literal types as
// `c.ctx.untypedStringId` (kind `UntypedString`, never `Prim`), so
// `"0123456789"[3:4]` typed `.invalid`. Lowering then dispatched `+` on that
// invalid type and emitted an integer `add` on the two string POINTERS
// instead of a `string_concat` call. `bit check` and `bit build` both
// exited 0 — the wrong code was silently accepted, and the program printed
// nothing instead of "3!". A variable receiver (`let d = "..."; d[3:4]`)
// was never affected, since a named local already carries a concrete type.
fn main() {
  print("0123456789"[3:4] + "!\n")
}
